    termsParagraph2: "<p class=\"muted\"><strong>Note: Virginia's notary commissioning requirements are changing on July 1, 2027.</strong> Newly enacted Code of Virginia § 47.1-5.2 (created by HB163/SB316, 2026 Acts of Assembly, cc. 364-365) will require new-commission applicants to complete a 4-hour course of instruction (including one hour on real estate fraud and financial exploitation of elderly persons) and pass a written examination to the satisfaction of the Secretary, and will require every recommission applicant to complete a 2-hour refresher course each time they recommission. Today, and for this practice bank, Virginia remains application-only: using this site's practice questions does not fulfill any Virginia notary education or examination requirement (current or future), and does not issue any official course-completion certificate, examination result, or notary commission — our content is a supplementary study aid only. Under the currently effective Code of Virginia Title 47.1 (the Virginia Notary Act), a Virginia notary commission is obtained by submitting a complete application and fee to the Secretary of the Commonwealth and taking an oath in person before the clerk of the circuit court where the applicant elects to qualify — no exam and no pre-commission course is required for a base commission today. Completing practice questions here also does not register you for, or schedule, that official process. While we strive to align our content with the current Code of Virginia Title 47.1 and the already-enacted July 1, 2027 amendments, it is provided \"as-is\" for self-study and does not constitute legal advice or a guaranteed outcome.</p>",
