    termsParagraph2: "<p class=\"muted\">Using this site's practice questions does not fulfill any part of West Virginia's notary public application process — including the statutory qualifications under §39-4-20(b), the sworn statement under §39-4-20(c), or the Secretary of State's online notary application system — and does not issue any official notary commission. West Virginia requires neither a mandatory training course nor a separate graded notary exam for its base commission; the Secretary of State issues a five-year commission directly to qualified applicants, and the statute's current text imposes no surety bond or other financial \"assurance\" requirement on the base commission. (The base application fee itself is set administratively by Secretary of State rule and is not a fixed dollar amount stated in the statute, so this content does not assert a specific figure for it.) Our content also covers West Virginia's separate Out-of-State Commissioner appointment under §39-4A-1 and §39-4A-2 — a distinct, optional 10-year appointment carrying its own statutory $500 nonrefundable fee, available only to notaries who already hold a base commission — but completing practice questions here does not apply for, or issue, that appointment either. While we strive to align our content with the current West Virginia Code §39-4-1 et seq. and §39-4A-1 et seq., it is provided \"as-is\" for self-study and does not constitute legal advice or a guaranteed outcome.</p>",
